#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063969 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1a59be3-afee-56bd-a554-037c5ad99f83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations When the Variables are Separate"}] :question/id #uuid "3b855590-1e40-5dee-a518-0211c627281e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13909" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $2x - y = 5y - z.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $x,$ so we will treat the other variables $y$ and $z$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $x.$\n\nFirst, we add $y$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2x - y &= 5y - z \\[5pt]\n\t\t2x - y + y &= 5y - z + y \\[5pt]\n\t\t2x &= 6y - z\n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we divide both sides by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2x &= 6y - z \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2x} 2 &= \\dfrac{6y - z} {2} \\[5pt]\n\t\tx &= \\dfrac{6y - z} {2}\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063970 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064194 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "202c3d83-dd90-539a-8e22-9689e936e308" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations When the Variables are Multiplied"}] :question/id #uuid "0fa3b7ba-5b22-5ba4-9898-0bdfee684683" :question/math-academy-id "e-13910" :question/problem "The energy $E$ of a body at rest is related to its mass $m$ by the equation $E=mC$, where $C$ is a constant. What is an expression for the mass ($m$) in terms of the energy ($E$) and the constant ($C$)?" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $m,$ so we will treat the other variables $E$ and $C$ as constants. Since $m$ is being multiplied by $C,$ we need to divide both sides of the equation by $C$:\n![](images/27/27683350a3eb25bd3962e12724a01f09350d4c62ba85afb137df8cff27992be7.svg)\nThus, $m = \\dfrac E C.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $C\\neq 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063971 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9f58b68-b706-5e6b-b2a4-851517a1db28" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Requiring Manipulation of Binomials"}] :question/id #uuid "6cf331f4-6251-53ff-889b-7762f381d087" :question/math-academy-id "e-13914" :question/problem "The total energy $E$ of a body moving with a (squared) velocity $V$ can be approximated using the equation $E = mC + \\dfrac{1}{2}mV.$ Find an expression for $V$ in terms of the other quantities." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $V,$ so we will treat the other variables $E,$ $C,$ and $m$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $V.$\n\nFirst, we subtract $mC$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =mC+\\frac{1}{2}mV \\\\ E-mC & =mC+\\frac{1}{2}mV-mC \\\\ E-mC & =\\frac{1}{2}mV\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $2$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E-mC & =\\frac{1}{2}mV \\\\ 2\\cdot (E-mC) & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}mV \\\\ 2(E-mC) & =mV\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide by $m$ to get $V$ alone:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(E-mC) & =mV \\\\ \\frac{2(E-mC)}{m} & =\\frac{mV}{m} \\\\ \\frac{2(E-mC)}{m} & =V\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $V = \\dfrac{2(E-mC)}{m}.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $m\\neq 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1a59be3-afee-56bd-a554-037c5ad99f83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations When the Variables are Separate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16950e72-b362-5e6f-88f9-2a640ed97951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\frac{m-2h}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488520} {:answer/id #uuid "753ef4e9-c18a-5f70-917d-3c3920d8ca21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\frac{2(h-m)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488702} {:answer/id #uuid "7deab777-c2dd-5601-b8e7-37ac5578cab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\frac{2}{3m-2h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488722} {:answer/id #uuid "e47626ff-f26d-54cd-a4ab-f41b3c4645bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-\\frac{m+h}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488948} {:answer/id #uuid "e809a959-a7e3-5017-bec0-23321eab7046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\frac{2(m+h)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e809a959-a7e3-5017-bec0-23321eab7046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\frac{2(m+h)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488955} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d356cb-97ed-53b6-8ae4-08e38186803d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8670623f-a7f4-578e-914d-6583efa2a94c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104505" :question/problem "Make $k$ the subject of the equation $-2h=2m-3k.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $k,$ so we will treat the other variables $h$ and $m$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $k.$\n\nFirst, we add $3k$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2h & =2m-3k \\\\ -2h+3k & =2m-3k+3k \\\\ 3k-2h & =2m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $2h$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k-2h & =2m \\\\ 3k-2h+2h & =2m+2h \\\\ 3k & =2m+2h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k & =2m+2h \\\\ \\frac{3k}{3} & =\\frac{2m+2h}{3} \\\\ k & =\\frac{2(m+h)}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1a59be3-afee-56bd-a554-037c5ad99f83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations When the Variables are Separate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088681b8-dece-5943-86ef-0ce140124469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y-5w}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "088681b8-dece-5943-86ef-0ce140124469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y-5w}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488489} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd35897b-af9b-538f-9da8-73a25cda3699" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ab827a9-09e7-59f8-93b4-f0c91318eeaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-215068" :question/problem "If $y=5w+7x,$ then $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $x,$ so we will treat the other variables $w$ and $y$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $x.$\n\nFirst, we subtract $5w$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5w+7x \\\\ y-5w & =5w+7x-5w \\\\ y-5w & =7x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x & =y-5w \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & =\\frac{y-5w}{7} \\\\ x & =\\frac{y-5w}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9f58b68-b706-5e6b-b2a4-851517a1db28" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Requiring Manipulation of Binomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79c8330d-6dc6-557a-9815-0dc1258b5bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2xz-4w}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79c8330d-6dc6-557a-9815-0dc1258b5bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2xz-4w}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488711} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef1b194-3723-50a3-b24b-91683d8db081" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de5d5814-9f69-514d-a764-4ee1edcf544a" :question/math-academy-id "q-215396" :question/problem "If $2xz-3y=4w,$ then $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $y,$ so we will treat the other variables $w,x,$ and $z$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $y.$\n\nFirst, we subtract $2xz$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xz-3y & =4w \\\\ 2xz-3y-2xz & =4w-2xz \\\\ -3y & =4w-2xz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $-3$ to get $y$ alone:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3y & =4w-2xz \\\\ \\frac{-3y}{-3} & =\\frac{4w-2xz}{-3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4w-2xz}{-3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{2xz-4w}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1a59be3-afee-56bd-a554-037c5ad99f83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations When the Variables are Separate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a235d76-c366-542d-9489-73cd1cbe8354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=9b+6a-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488748} {:answer/id #uuid "90beab16-0f3f-5729-9a4f-2ff5756dab6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=\\frac{9b-6a}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488760} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f48295-a359-57d7-a83b-45f2b69c8918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=9b+6a+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488823} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c54d08-3027-5477-8e6e-eddd2f2d7c2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=9b+3a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488865} {:answer/id #uuid "ce723e6b-a7f1-5c83-93c0-26b1992c7d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=\\frac{9b+6a}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce723e6b-a7f1-5c83-93c0-26b1992c7d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=\\frac{9b+6a}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488892} :answer-field/id #uuid "892f2275-2bc5-559b-b11e-c37f8b2dc7b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8065c427-1ef3-5709-a373-7fc538897b32" :question/math-academy-id "q-44312" :question/problem "Make $c$ the subject of the equation $-6a=9b-2c.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $c,$ so we will treat the other variables $a$ and $b$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $c.$\n\nFirst, we add $2c$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6a & =9b-2c \\\\ -6a+2c & =9b-2c+2c \\\\ -6a+2c & =9b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $6a$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6a+2c & =9b \\\\ -6a+2c+6a & =9b+6a \\\\ 2c & =9b+6a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c & =9b+6a \\\\ \\frac{2c}{2} & =\\frac{9b+6a}{2} \\\\ c & =\\frac{9b+6a}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9f58b68-b706-5e6b-b2a4-851517a1db28" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Requiring Manipulation of Binomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0776833c-e0c2-532d-b051-2d003c8e095a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C=PQ-E" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488486} {:answer/id #uuid "39705b79-f9e1-5882-96bb-64867d45e168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P=Q(E+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488588} {:answer/id #uuid "65f5c1ef-d90e-568c-bb69-d1d84bbf05ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P=\\frac{E+C}{Q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488664} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a6184b-cc9c-5368-98bb-fcc102c67792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P=\\frac{E-C}{Q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488842} {:answer/id #uuid "c8133753-993a-528a-abab-a54fb3d9628e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q=\\frac{E+C}{P}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65f5c1ef-d90e-568c-bb69-d1d84bbf05ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P=\\frac{E+C}{Q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488664} :answer-field/id #uuid "7599f77d-da1f-5c75-baeb-b3ad971719bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "220b7284-89df-58d8-b7fb-14c6a632a4f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-44850" :question/problem "In business, the economic profit is defined as\n\n$$\nE=PQ-C,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is the sale price of a good, $Q$ is the number of goods sold, and $C$ is the cost of producing $Q$ goods. \n\nWhat is the formula for the price in terms of the other variables?" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $P,$ so we will treat the other variables $E,$ $Q,$ and $C$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $P.$\n\nFirst, we add $C$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =PQ-C \\\\ E+C & =PQ-C+C \\\\ E+C & =PQ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $Q$ to get $P$ alone:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E+C & =PQ \\\\ \\frac{E+C}{Q} & =\\frac{PQ}{Q} \\\\ \\frac{E+C}{Q} & =P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $P=\\frac{E+C}{Q}.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $Q\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1a59be3-afee-56bd-a554-037c5ad99f83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations When the Variables are Separate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256c4e0a-88bd-5462-b34b-8e0201196256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{c-7b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488548} {:answer/id #uuid "767ff254-9406-539d-9543-9df59a37d2cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{c-7b}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488704} {:answer/id #uuid "87e00313-7fa3-5ad4-b7f0-80d16185aea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=3c-7b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488737} {:answer/id #uuid "8a19160c-90ed-5621-b707-63db2d30d54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=c-7b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488747} {:answer/id #uuid "fad7c0e4-a858-510b-8bfb-0a65e5cb5862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=3c-21b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "767ff254-9406-539d-9543-9df59a37d2cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{c-7b}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488704} :answer-field/id #uuid "d99aa76c-d573-53bf-9199-ad883ba2b9fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16ef2d7f-f052-5e96-9861-83b3cce4c0bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-6272" :question/problem "Solve for $a$ where $c=3a+7b.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $a,$ so we will treat the other variables $b$ and $c$ constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $a.$\n\nFirst, we subtract $7b$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =3a+7b \\\\ c-7b & =3a+7b-7b \\\\ c-7b & =3a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a & =c-7b \\\\ \\frac{3a}{3} & =\\frac{c-7b}{3} \\\\ a & =\\frac{c-7b}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064194 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "202c3d83-dd90-539a-8e22-9689e936e308" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations When the Variables are Multiplied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66ea85cf-8a45-52b0-afc6-5302893f8c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=\\frac{d}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488667} {:answer/id #uuid "71975e50-0db1-56f1-b56c-2415d3dc4ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=\\frac{t}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488698} {:answer/id #uuid "d1565845-fb01-5dfd-baa4-d20077d7f91a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=d\\cdot t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488898} {:answer/id #uuid "d69843dc-5143-515e-8912-eb43f77877dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=d+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488912} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3022ea-5ac3-50d9-99da-0805732230e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=d-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66ea85cf-8a45-52b0-afc6-5302893f8c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=\\frac{d}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488667} :answer-field/id #uuid "72ae3726-b8b0-5976-82de-d20849cd73be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14cd0a28-442e-5455-9aaf-cc966e4aa8e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-67262" :question/problem "The formula to calculate the distance that an object in uniform rectilinear motion has traveled is given by\n\n$$\nd=v\\cdot t,\n$$\n\nwhere $v$ represents the velocity of the moving object and $t$ represents time.\n\nWhat is the formula for velocity in terms of distance and time?" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $v,$ so we will treat the other variables $d$ and $t$ as constants. Since $v$ is being multiplied by $t,$ we need to divide both sides of the equation by $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =v\\cdot t \\\\ \\frac{d}{t} & =\\frac{v\\cdot t}{t} \\\\ \\frac{d}{t} & =\\frac{v\\cdot \\cancel{t}}{\\cancel{t}} \\\\ \\frac{d}{t} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $v=\\frac{d}{t}.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $t\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064194 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "202c3d83-dd90-539a-8e22-9689e936e308" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations When the Variables are Multiplied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44a39803-ff77-57fe-b5c2-efc3892518ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=\\frac{1}{2A\\pi r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488610} {:answer/id #uuid "509ef183-713a-5b80-9cea-d36045a8b4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=\\frac{A}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488625} {:answer/id #uuid "5f309670-2000-5f40-9e63-064e5228ef92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=\\frac{A}{\\pi r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488653} {:answer/id #uuid "b984c89f-22b6-5f39-a9e4-709f228a20bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=\\frac{A}{2\\pi r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488840} {:answer/id #uuid "dd8c229b-04e9-5db8-b9b0-7619b9b60f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=\\frac{2A}{\\pi r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b984c89f-22b6-5f39-a9e4-709f228a20bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=\\frac{A}{2\\pi r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488840} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d4cbd72-7cba-578e-b4d1-9a4441fcaa2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "237b013e-fcd6-5777-9bce-b39ac50c9f8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-75547" :question/problem "The surface area of a cylinder is given by \n\n$$\nA=2\\pi rh\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius and $h$ is the height of the cylinder.\n\nWhat is the formula for the height of the cylinder in terms of the other variables?" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $h,$ so we will treat the other variables $A$ and $r$ as constants.\n\nFirst, we rearrange and then divide both sides by $2\\pi r$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2\\pi rh \\\\ A & =(2\\pi r)h \\\\ \\frac{A}{2\\pi r} & =\\frac{(2\\pi r)h}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{A}{2\\pi r} & =h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h=\\frac{A}{2\\pi r}.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $r\\ne 0.$"}]}
